1. Core Hypothesis & Theoretical InsightsOur research addresses the fundamental limits of Information Bottleneck Theory in edge computing. When high-resolution medical features are aggressively compressed to ultra-low dimensions ($d = 4$ or $8$) to save transmission bandwidth, classical classifiers experience topological collapse—the data points overlap in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$, making classification hyperplanes impossible to draw.Our core hypothesis consists of two competing geometric phenomena:Latent Reshaping: By mapping the compressed vector into a $2^n$-dimensional complex Hilbert space ($\mathbb{C}^{2^n}$), a Variational Quantum Circuit (VQC) utilizes its high effective dimension and Fourier expressivity to resolve this entanglement. When trained end-to-end, the backpropagated quantum gradients actively reshape the terminal classical convolutional filters into a quantum-friendly topology, bypassing classical performance plateaus.The Precision Paradox: The very mechanism that grants the VQC its expressivity—precise, high-frequency trigonometric phase interference—renders it an algorithmic "Glass Cannon." In data-scarce environments, the circuit fits the training points using steep, high-frequency phase landscapes. Minor analog sensor noise causes massive phase misalignment, destroying the interference patterns and causing a catastrophic performance crash. Crucially, we hypothesize that data abundance acts as a topological regularizer, forcing the quantum gradients to construct broad, stable minima that neutralize this fragility.2. Expected Behavioral PatternsAcross Different DatasetsBreastMNIST (Ultrasound - Texture Ambiguity): This modality features low contrast and fuzzy boundaries. Classical models struggle heavily under tight bottlenecks here. We expect this dataset to showcase our most dramatic Latent Reshaping effect, as the quantum head forces the backbone to radically alter its spatial filters to find separability.PneumoniaMNIST (Chest X-Rays - Structural Modality): X-rays have rigid macro-structures (ribs, lung fields). Classical models handle this better even under compression. The "Bottleneck Gap" will be narrower here, but it will serve as an excellent baseline to prove that the Precision Paradox is an algorithmic property of the quantum head, not a quirk of a single dataset.OrganMNIST (Abdominal CT Slices - Multi-Class Geometry): CT scans represent dense, highly structural grayscale information. Expanding into this dataset tests whether our 4-variable bottleneck can compress multi-organ spatial relationships and if the VQC can resolve multi-class separation boundaries that completely paralyze classical linear layers.BloodMNIST (Microscopy - High-Variance Shapes): Individual blood cells have high geometric variance in shape and boundary. This will be the ultimate testing ground for the Precision Paradox, as minor pixel-level noise will significantly shift the cell boundary features, letting us map exactly how fast quantum phase misalignment triggers under structural variations.Across Qubit Scaling ($4$ vs. $8$ vs. $16$ Qubits)$4$ Qubits (The Baseline): Highly parameter-efficient, completely immune to barren plateaus, and shows a distinct, clean performance advantage under severe data scarcity.$8$ Qubits (The Sweet Spot & The Cliff): Compressing the bottleneck to 8 dimensions expands our quantum Hilbert space to $\mathbb{C}^{256}$. In pristine environments, this will widen the quantum advantage gap significantly. However, we expect to encounter the onset of Barren Plateaus (vanishing gradients) during random initialization. We will deploy Identity Initialization to rescue gradient flow. Furthermore, under noise, the Precision Paradox will accelerate—because more qubits unlock even higher-frequency Fourier components, the model will become ultra-sensitive, misaligning at lower noise levels ($\sigma < 0.03$).$16$ Qubits (The Algorithmic Boundary Wall): Simulating 16 qubits expands the state vector to a complex space of $65,536$ dimensions. The parameter-shift rule will require $192$ unique circuit evaluations per backward pass. We expect severe gradient saturation due to deep barren plateaus. By demonstrating that the 16-qubit hybrid model flatlines or fails to optimize, we will empirically establish the practical scalability ceiling for unapproximated edge QML architectures.3. Justification Document for RVCE ProfessorsYou can present the following text directly to your professors and office to justify the journal upgrade and secure your dedicated access to the Tesla A100 GPU.Executive Summary: Expansion of Hybrid QML Research for Q1 Journal SubmissionProject Title: Expressivity and Robustness Trade-offs in Edge-Constrained Hybrid Quantum Neural NetworksObjective: To expand our verified conference-level findings on "Latent Reshaping" and the "Precision Paradox" into an exhaustive, multi-dimensional study meeting the peer-review standards of top-tier Q1 journals (e.g., IEEE Access or IEEE Transactions on Medical Imaging).Core Enhancements for the Journal Framework:Multi-Modal Clinical Benchmarking: We are expanding our evaluation from two datasets to four distinct medical imaging modalities (BreastMNIST, PneumoniaMNIST, OrganMNIST, and BloodMNIST). This eliminates data bias and proves our algorithmic discoveries apply universally across Ultrasound, X-Ray, CT, and Microscopy.Algorithmic vs. Compression Baselines: To rigorously defend our "Quantum Advantage" claim against standard classical deep learning, we are integrating advanced classical dimensionality reduction baselines, explicitly benchmarking our Variational Quantum Circuit against Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and a Non-Linear Classical Autoencoder under identical bottleneck constraints.Mathematical Grounding of Phase Misalignment: We are introducing an automated Fourier Spectrum Analyzer to extract the trained circuit's frequency coefficients, mathematically proving that data scarcity forces the quantum head to rely on high-frequency, noise-fragile trigonometric sines.Granular Data Scarcity Sweeps: To pinpoint the exact tipping point where data abundance transforms into a topological regularizer, we are increasing our data fraction evaluation resolution across a 6-tier gradient: $1\%$, $5\%$, $10\%$, $25\%$, $50\%$, and $100\%$.Statistical Validation: To eliminate architectural abnormalities and smooth out quantum optimization variances, the entire experimental suite will be scaled from 3 random seeds to a 5-seed ensemble, backed by Welch’s t-test $p$-value metrics.Quantum Scaling & Boundary Mapping ($4$, $8$, and $16$ Qubits): We are mapping the exact limits of hybrid scalability. We will benchmark how expanding the information bottleneck to 8 and 16 qubits triggers quantum gradient saturation (Barren Plateaus), establishing the practical boundary conditions for deploying unapproximated PQCs on low-power edge medical devices.import os
import torch
import numpy as np
import medmnist
from torchvision import transforms
from torch.utils.data import DataLoader, Subset
from typing import Tuple

def get_medmnist_loaders(
    dataset_name: str, 
    batch_size: int = 32, 
    train_frac: float = 1.0, 
    data_root: str = "data",
    seed: int = 42
) -> Tuple[DataLoader, DataLoader, DataLoader]:
    """
    Universal MedMNIST loader for QML Journal Expansion.
    Dynamically handles both 1-channel (Grayscale) and 3-channel (RGB) datasets
    while adapting them for the ResNet-18 feature extractor.
    """
    np.random.seed(seed)
    torch.manual_seed(seed)
    os.makedirs(data_root, exist_ok=True)

    # Resolve dataset info to check channels
    info = medmnist.INFO[dataset_name]
    DataClass = getattr(medmnist, info['python_class'])
    n_channels = info['n_channels']

    # Dynamically build transformations based on image channels
    transform_list = [
        transforms.ToTensor(),
    ]
    
    # Only repeat channels if the image is grayscale
    if n_channels == 1:
        transform_list.append(transforms.Lambda(lambda x: x.repeat(3, 1, 1)))
        
    transform_list.extend([
        transforms.Resize((224, 224), antialias=True),
        transforms.Normalize(mean=[0.485, 0.456, 0.406], std=[0.229, 0.224, 0.225])
    ])
    
    data_transform = transforms.Compose(transform_list)

    # Load datasets
    train_dataset = DataClass(split='train', transform=data_transform, download=True, root=data_root)
    val_dataset   = DataClass(split='val', transform=data_transform, download=True, root=data_root)
    test_dataset  = DataClass(split='test', transform=data_transform, download=True, root=data_root)
    
    # Enforce data scarcity regime
    if train_frac < 1.0:
        total_len = len(train_dataset)
        n_samples = max(int(total_len * train_frac), batch_size) 
        
        indices = np.random.choice(total_len, n_samples, replace=False)
        train_dataset = Subset(train_dataset, indices)
        print(f"[{dataset_name}] Applied {train_frac*100}% data scarcity. Samples: {n_samples}")
    else:
        print(f"[{dataset_name}] Utilizing full 100% dataset. Samples: {len(train_dataset)}")
    
    # Construct DataLoaders
    train_loader = DataLoader(train_dataset, batch_size=batch_size, shuffle=True)
    val_loader   = DataLoader(val_dataset, batch_size=batch_size, shuffle=False)
    test_loader  = DataLoader(test_dataset, batch_size=batch_size, shuffle=False)
    
    return train_loader, val_loader, test_loader


if __name__ == "__main__":
    # Test and cache all 6 datasets for the Journal run
    datasets = [
        'breastmnist', 'pneumoniamnist', # The Conference baselines (1-channel)
        'bloodmnist', 'pathmnist',       # New Journal datasets (3-channel)
        'dermamnist', 'octmnist'         # New Journal datasets (3-channel / 1-channel)
    ]
    
    os.makedirs("/home/jovyan/qml_exp_2026/data_cache", exist_ok=True)
    
    for ds in datasets:
        print(f"\n--- Processing {ds} ---")
        train_dl, val_dl, test_dl = get_medmnist_loaders(
            dataset_name=ds, 
            batch_size=16, 
            train_frac=0.1,  # Testing the scarcity fraction
            data_root="/home/jovyan/qml_exp_2026/data_cache"
        )
        
        imgs, labels = next(iter(train_dl))
        print(f"[Success] {ds} Output Shape: {imgs.shape} (Should be Bx3x224x224)")
